{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n27 \n \n \n\u2022 \nIn the spherical, infinite domain configuration, the characteristic time for the diffusion-\ncontrolled system to effectively reach steady-state is found to be on the order of 105 s.  \n\u2022 \nRadiative extinction is observed when, at constant reactant flow rate, a flame expands \nuntil radiation heat loss approaches the chemical heat release. Before a flame \nexperiences complete extinction, it may enter an oscillatory mode in which part of the \nflame partially extinguishes and then reforms. The oscillations grow in magnitude until \ncomplete extinction. \n \nE. Accomplishment of Approved Objectives  \nAll the approved objectives, i.e., not designated as desired or highly desired in Section 2.C, were \naccomplished. \n \nF. Additional Objectives Achieved \nSee 4.D, Surprises and New Discoveries, above. \n \nG. Impacts, Benefits, and Products \nThe overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion \nof hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, \nsoot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must \nbe controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to \nreduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but \nthese techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial \nperformance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion \nto address these concerns. The approach is termed \u201cFlame Design\u201d as it attempts to take our \nfundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on \nthe problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. \n \nH. Patents \nNo patents have been filed from this research. \n \n \n5. Follow-On Experiments \nThe range of available experimental conditions was limited, including the fuel and oxygen concentrations \nand the ambient pressure.  In particular, for safety reasons, the O2 mole fraction limit for normal tests was \n40% and the C2H4 mole fraction limit for inverse flames was 65%.  Tests with CH4 as fuel and CO2 as diluent, \nwere slated for the test matrix by the investigators, but not allowed.  Finally, co-flow burner tests at the \nconditions considered by this project could be especially valuable.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 27}}